{"kind":"artifact","text":"notes/2026-10-04-authority-effects-and-publication.md at exact 4ef1a5e37baebb82854e7b33340f31bd0e05237a on request/authority-r20, under promise git:sha1…6074ebc on request git:sha1…b601df2\n\nAuthority, effects and publication, revision 20 at 4ef1a5e37baebb82854e7b33340f31bd0e05237a on request/authority-r20, for request 837943bb. It is the successor of adopted revision 18: it holds revision 19's content (c3f2f103) and the repairs of the checker's changes-requested verdict d2068c05 on it, and two further things. The facts file is unchanged. Custody repair 1: a person (controller or admin under task.control, never the worker's key) may save the current fenced root: save gains a field fenced that must equal the records; every attempt of that instance must already have an outcome, so the cut names no attempt, settles no unknown effect and releases no pin; the registration stays fenced; it gives no tool, no write, nothing under the old hold, no push and no export. The claim that one more save is always admitted is replaced by its conditions, and six states are listed where dirty work exists and nobody can save it, each with what would close it; the widest is a worker's register over a fenced unsaved registration, for which a guard is asked of the recovery design. Custody repair 2: a cut has a member final (every process ended or frozen before the read and none thawed after); a commit fences and opens destruction only on a final cut; unsaved is false only on a final post-stop cut; a non-final cut still commits a checkpoint shown as saved as of its cut time and ends no host. Until the recovery design and IA state the supervisor's side, the rule is that no cut is final and no commit ends a host. The planner's answer on overdue hosts is applied as given and marked as a policy answer distinct from both repairs: end-host names the rule applied, is signed by an admin key under task.control (my mapping of the installation's controller; confirm or leave